---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import DiagramFlow from '../components/diagrams/DiagramFlow.astro';
import DiagramBox from '../components/diagrams/DiagramBox.astro';
import DiagramArrow from '../components/diagrams/DiagramArrow.astro';
import DiagramStack from '../components/diagrams/DiagramStack.astro';
import DiagramLayer from '../components/diagrams/DiagramLayer.astro';
import DiagramTag from '../components/diagrams/DiagramTag.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<BaseLayout title="User Guide — Town OS">
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <h1>User Guide</h1>
      <p class="page-lead">
        A practical walkthrough of Town OS — from writing a USB drive to reporting bugs.
        Whether you're a first-time user or a developer building packages, this guide
        covers everything you need to get started.
      </p>
      <nav class="toc">
        <ul>
          <li><a href="#quick-start">Quick Start</a></li>
          <li><a href="#building-the-usb-image">Building the USB Image from Source</a></li>
          <li><a href="#sledgehammer">Sledgehammer Boot Option</a></li>
          <li><a href="#dns-setup">Setting Up DNS on Your Router</a></li>
          <li><a href="#trusting-the-ca">Trusting the Certificate Authority</a></li>
          <li><a href="#networks">Networks and Remote Access</a></li>
          <li><a href="#android-app">Town OS on Android</a></li>
          <li><a href="#building-vms">Building VMs from USB Images</a></li>
          <li><a href="#raid-installation">RAID Installation</a></li>
          <li><a href="#using-the-ui">Using the User Interface</a></li>
          <li><a href="#pages">Hosting Static Sites</a></li>
          <li><a href="#updating">Updating Town OS</a></li>
          <li><a href="#building-a-package">Building a Package</a></li>
          <li><a href="#self-hosting-ideas">Things You Can Self-Host</a></li>
          <li><a href="#dev-environment">Development Environment and Test Suite</a></li>
          <li><a href="#reporting-bugs">Reporting Bugs</a></li>
        </ul>
      </nav>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Quick Start -->
  <section class="section quickstart-section">
    <div class="container">
      <h2 class="section-title" id="quick-start">Quick Start</h2>
      <p class="doc-text" style="font-weight: 700; color: #e0e0e0; font-size: 1.1rem;">
        Get Town OS running on a spare computer in minutes. All you need is a USB-C drive
        (4 GB or larger) and a Linux machine to write it from.
      </p>

      <h3>1. Write the USB Drive</h3>
      <p class="doc-text">
        Run the installer script on any Linux machine with <code>podman</code>,
        <code>curl</code>, <code>bzip2</code>, <code>dd</code>, <code>lsblk</code>, and
        <code>tar</code> installed — <strong>Podman is required</strong>, since the script
        pulls the installer image with it (and must be working, not just installed). It
        downloads the latest Town OS image and writes it directly to a USB drive.
      </p>
      <pre><code>curl -sSLO https://town-os.github.io/install.sh && bash install.sh</code></pre>
      <p class="doc-text">
        The script will scan for connected USB devices, show you a list with device names
        and sizes, and ask you to pick one. After you confirm, it streams the compressed
        image and writes it to the drive in a single pass. The whole process takes a few
        minutes depending on your internet connection.
      </p>

      <p class="doc-text">
        <strong>Raspberry Pi (4 / 400 / CM4, 5 / CM5):</strong> pass <code>RPI=1</code> to
        write the native-boot Raspberry Pi image to an SD card, USB drive, or NVMe instead
        of the standard PC image:
      </p>
      <pre><code>curl -sSLO https://town-os.github.io/install.sh && RPI=1 bash install.sh</code></pre>
      <p class="doc-text">
        The Pi image is always 64-bit Arm and boots without UEFI, so it works even when you
        run the installer from an x86_64 machine. A Raspberry Pi boots straight from the
        inserted card/drive with no boot menu; for Pi 5 NVMe boot, make sure the bootloader
        EEPROM boot order includes NVMe (set it with <code>rpi-eeprom-config</code>).
      </p>

      <div class="data-warning">
        <div class="data-warning-badge">DATA DESTRUCTION WARNING</div>
        <p class="data-warning-text">
          Town OS will <strong>format and use all detected local storage devices</strong>
          (NVMe, SATA, SAS, SD cards). Booting Town OS on a machine will
          <strong>permanently destroy all existing data</strong> on every internal drive.
        </p>
        <p class="data-warning-text">
          Only boot Town OS on a dedicated machine with no data you want to keep, or use
          the <a href="#building-vms">VM instructions</a> to try it safely first — including
          <a href="#qemu-usb"><code>make qemu-usb</code></a>, which boots the stick you just
          wrote inside QEMU (read-only) without touching any real disk.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h3>2. Boot from USB</h3>
      <p class="doc-text">
        Plug the USB drive into the target computer and boot from it. You may need to
        press a key during startup to reach the boot menu — common keys are
        <strong>F12</strong>, <strong>F2</strong>, <strong>Esc</strong>, or
        <strong>Del</strong>, depending on your hardware.
      </p>

      <h3>3. First Boot: Use Sledgehammer</h3>
      <p class="doc-text">
        If the target machine has been used before for something else, <strong>select the sledgehammer
        boot option</strong> from the boot menu on your first boot. Sledgehammer wipes
        all detected storage devices and ensures Town OS starts with a completely clean
        slate. This avoids problems caused by leftover partition tables, filesystems,
        or RAID metadata from a previous operating system.
      </p>
      <p class="doc-text">
        After sledgehammer finishes, the machine reboots automatically and Town OS
        performs a fresh storage setup. See
        <a href="#sledgehammer">Sledgehammer Boot Option</a> for full details.
      </p>

      <h3>4. What Happens on Boot</h3>
      <p class="doc-text">
        Town OS loads entirely into RAM from the USB drive, then launches
        <a href="https://github.com/erikh/ttyforce" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ttyforce</a>,
        an interactive TUI installer that guides you through setup directly on the
        machine's console.
      </p>
      <ul class="doc-list">
        <li><strong>Network configuration</strong> — ttyforce detects available interfaces. If a wired connection with link is present, it advances automatically. Otherwise it presents WiFi network selection with signal strength, security info, and WPA2/WPA3 password entry.</li>
        <li><strong>Disk provisioning</strong> — ttyforce groups detected disks by type and size, then automatically selects the appropriate <a href="#raid-installation">RAID level</a>: single drive for 1 disk, RAID1 (mirror) for 2, or RAID5 (striped with parity) for 3 or more. All storage uses btrfs.</li>
        <li><strong>SSH key import</strong> — you can enter GitHub usernames to import public SSH keys for secure remote access.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="doc-text">
        After provisioning completes, the system reboots and ttyforce switches to
        getty mode — a live status display showing service health, system metrics,
        and journal output on the console. From here you can log in, reconfigure
        networking, or trigger a sledgehammer wipe.
      </p>

      <h3>5. Create Your Account and Start Using It</h3>
      <p class="doc-text">
        From any device on the same network, open a browser and go to
        <code>http://town-os.local</code>. If that doesn't work, check your router's
        DHCP client list for a device named "town-os" and use its IP address directly.
        You will be prompted to create an administrator account. Once logged in, you can
        install packages, manage storage, and configure services from the dashboard.
      </p>

      <h3>6. Point Your Router's DNS at Town OS</h3>
      <p class="doc-text">
        Town OS ships with <a href="https://gitea.com/town-os/rolodex" target="_blank" rel="noopener">rolodex</a>,
        a DNS server that resolves package hostnames and forwards everything else upstream.
        To make every device on your network use it automatically, give the Town OS machine
        a static IP (or DHCP reservation) and set your router's <strong>Primary DNS Server</strong>
        to that address. See <a href="#dns-setup">Setting Up DNS on Your Router</a> for
        per-brand instructions and verification steps.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Creating the USB Drive -->
  <section class="section alt-section">
    <div class="container">
      <h2 class="section-title" id="building-the-usb-image">Building the USB Image from Source</h2>
      <p class="doc-text">
        The Town OS USB image is built from the
        <a href="https://gitea.com/town-os/install" target="_blank" rel="noopener">install</a>
        repository. The build process produces a bootable image with a squashfs root filesystem
        and a GPT partition layout.
      </p>

      <h3>Prerequisites</h3>
      <p class="doc-text">
        You need a Linux host and a USB drive (4 GB or larger). The build uses Arch
        Linux tooling (<code>pacstrap</code>, <code>mkinitcpio</code>, <code>arch-chroot</code>),
        but you do <strong>not</strong> need to be running Arch — on any other distro
        <code>make image</code> automatically runs the build inside a same-architecture
        Arch container. Install the host dependencies with the bundled target for your
        distro:
      </p>
      <ul class="doc-list">
        <li><code>make deps</code> — Arch (and derivatives) or Fedora/RHEL</li>
        <li><code>make deps-debian</code> — Debian or Ubuntu</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="doc-text">
        These pull in everything the build and VM tooling need — <code>make</code>,
        <code>podman</code>, <code>arch-install-scripts</code>, <code>squashfs-tools</code>,
        <code>parted</code>, <code>e2fsprogs</code>, <code>dosfstools</code>,
        <code>qemu</code>, and <code>libvirt</code>.
      </p>

      <h3>Cloning and Building</h3>
      <pre><code>git clone https://gitea.com/town-os/install.git
cd install
make image</code></pre>
      <p class="doc-text">
        Running <code>make</code> or <code>make image</code> builds the complete USB image.
        The process downloads the base system, installs Town OS components, compresses
        everything into a squashfs filesystem, and assembles the final disk image with
        a GPT partition table.
      </p>

      <h3>Partitioning and Squashfs</h3>
      <p class="doc-text">
        The resulting image uses a GPT layout with:
      </p>
      <ul class="doc-list">
        <li>A BIOS boot partition (1 MiB) for legacy BIOS booting</li>
        <li>An EFI system partition (64 MiB, FAT32) for UEFI booting</li>
        <li>A data partition (ext4) containing the squashfs root filesystem and GRUB</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="doc-text">
        At boot time, Town OS mounts the squashfs as a read-only lower layer, with a
        tmpfs overlay on top. This means the OS runs entirely in RAM — the USB drive
        itself is only read at boot. All runtime changes happen in memory and are
        discarded on reboot, giving you a clean slate every time.
      </p>

      <h3>Writing the Image</h3>
      <p class="doc-text">
        The simplest way is <code>make flash</code>, which builds the image if it is
        stale and writes it to a USB device. To do it by hand, note that the build
        produces a dated, arch-suffixed file (e.g.
        <code>town-os-2026-07-25-x86_64.img</code>) — write it with <code>dd</code>:
      </p>
      <pre><code># Find your USB device (e.g. /dev/sdb)
lsblk

# Write the image (replace the filename and /dev/sdX with your device)
sudo dd if=town-os-YYYY-MM-DD-x86_64.img of=/dev/sdX bs=4M status=progress conv=fsync</code></pre>
      <p class="doc-text callout">
        Double-check the target device — <code>dd</code> will overwrite whatever you point it at
        without confirmation.
      </p>

      <DiagramFlow title="Boot Process">
        <DiagramBox label="USB Drive" sub="GPT Partition Table" variant="purple">
          <DiagramTag variant="purple">EFI System</DiagramTag>
          <DiagramTag variant="purple">squashfs Root</DiagramTag>
        </DiagramBox>
        <DiagramArrow label="boot" />
        <DiagramStack>
          <DiagramLayer variant="top">Running System</DiagramLayer>
          <DiagramLayer variant="teal" badge="read-write">tmpfs overlay</DiagramLayer>
          <DiagramLayer variant="purple" badge="read-only">squashfs</DiagramLayer>
        </DiagramStack>
        <DiagramArrow />
        <DiagramBox label="RAM" sub="OS runs entirely in memory" variant="teal" />
      </DiagramFlow>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Sledgehammer Boot Option -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <h2 class="section-title" id="sledgehammer">Sledgehammer Boot Option</h2>
      <p class="doc-text">
        The Town OS USB drive includes a <strong>sledgehammer</strong> boot option that
        wipes all detected storage devices and resets the system to a clean state. This is
        useful when you want to start fresh — for example, after testing, before
        redeploying to new hardware, or when storage has become corrupted.
      </p>

      <h3>What It Does</h3>
      <p class="doc-text">
        When you select the sledgehammer option at boot, Town OS will:
      </p>
      <ul class="doc-list">
        <li>Detect all local storage devices (NVMe, SATA/SAS, SD cards) — the same detection used during normal boot</li>
        <li>Wipe the partition tables and filesystems on every detected device</li>
        <li>Reboot the system, which then performs a fresh storage setup as if it were the first boot</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="doc-text callout">
        Sledgehammer destroys all data on all detected storage devices. The USB drive itself
        is not affected — only the internal disks. Make sure you have backups of anything
        you need before using this option.
      </p>

      <h3>How to Use It</h3>
      <ol class="doc-list">
        <li>Plug the Town OS USB drive into the target machine and boot from it</li>
        <li>At the boot menu, select the <strong>sledgehammer</strong> entry instead of the default boot option</li>
        <li>The system will wipe all detected storage and reboot automatically</li>
        <li>On the next boot, Town OS will set up storage from scratch using your <code>town-os.yaml</code> configuration</li>
      </ol>

      <h3>When to Use It</h3>
      <ul class="doc-list">
        <li><strong>Factory reset</strong> — return the system to a clean state as if it were freshly deployed</li>
        <li><strong>Storage backend change</strong> — switching from btrfs to ZFS (or vice versa) requires clearing existing storage first</li>
        <li><strong>Corrupted storage</strong> — if the filesystem is damaged beyond repair, sledgehammer gives you a clean starting point</li>
        <li><strong>Redeployment</strong> — repurposing hardware for a different Town OS configuration</li>
      </ul>

      <DiagramFlow title="Sledgehammer Reset Flow">
        <DiagramBox label="Boot Menu" sub="Select sledgehammer" variant="neutral">
          <DiagramTag>sledgehammer</DiagramTag>
        </DiagramBox>
        <DiagramArrow label="wipe" />
        <DiagramBox label="Clear Storage" sub="All detected disks wiped" variant="purple">
          <DiagramTag variant="purple">NVMe</DiagramTag>
          <DiagramTag variant="purple">SATA</DiagramTag>
          <DiagramTag variant="purple">SD</DiagramTag>
        </DiagramBox>
        <DiagramArrow label="reboot" />
        <DiagramBox label="Fresh Setup" sub="Storage configured from scratch" variant="teal">
          <DiagramTag variant="teal">town-os.yaml</DiagramTag>
        </DiagramBox>
      </DiagramFlow>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- DNS Setup -->
  <section class="section alt-section">
    <div class="container">
      <h2 class="section-title" id="dns-setup">Setting Up DNS on Your Router</h2>
      <p class="doc-text">
        Town OS includes <a href="https://gitea.com/town-os/rolodex" target="_blank" rel="noopener">rolodex</a>,
        a DNS server that manages authoritative zones for your packages and forwards
        upstream queries. To use it as your network's DNS server,
        you need to tell your router to hand out the Town OS machine's IP address as the
        DNS server for your network. This way every device on your network automatically
        uses rolodex for DNS — no per-device configuration needed.
      </p>

      <h3>Find Your Town OS IP Address</h3>
      <p class="doc-text">
        You'll need the local IP address of your Town OS machine. You can find it by:
      </p>
      <ul class="doc-list">
        <li>Logging in to the dashboard at <code>http://town-os.local</code> after first-boot setup and selecting the <strong>internal IP</strong> shown on the dashboard — this is the address to use as your DNS server</li>
        <li>Running <code>make vm-ip</code> if you're using a VM</li>
        <li>Checking your router's DHCP client list for a device named "town-os"</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="screenshot-ref">
        <img src={`${base}images/screenshots/dashboard-internal-ip.webp`} alt="Town OS dashboard showing External IP and Internal IP with copy buttons" width="2296" height="866" loading="lazy" />
      </div>
      <p class="doc-text">
        For DNS to work reliably, your Town OS machine should have a static IP address
        or a DHCP reservation on your router. If the IP changes, DNS will break for
        your whole network.
      </p>

      <h3>Assign a Static IP or DHCP Reservation</h3>
      <p class="doc-text">
        Most routers let you reserve an IP address for a specific device based on its
        MAC address. This is usually found under <strong>LAN Settings</strong>,
        <strong>DHCP</strong>, or <strong>Address Reservation</strong>. Find the Town OS
        machine in the client list and reserve its current IP.
      </p>
      <p class="doc-text">
        If your router doesn't support DHCP reservations, you can configure a static
        IP on the Town OS machine itself by adding it to <code>town-os.yaml</code>.
      </p>

      <h3>Change the DNS Server in Your Router</h3>
      <p class="doc-text">
        The exact steps vary by router brand, but the general process is the same:
      </p>
      <ol class="doc-list">
        <li>Log in to your router's admin interface (usually <code>192.168.1.1</code> or <code>192.168.0.1</code>)</li>
        <li>Find the <strong>DHCP Settings</strong>, <strong>LAN Settings</strong>, or <strong>DNS Settings</strong> section</li>
        <li>Change the <strong>Primary DNS Server</strong> to your Town OS machine's IP address</li>
        <li>Optionally set a <strong>Secondary DNS Server</strong> as a fallback (e.g. <code>1.1.1.1</code> or <code>8.8.8.8</code>) — this will be used if Town OS is unreachable</li>
        <li>Save and apply the settings</li>
      </ol>
      <p class="doc-text">
        After saving, devices on your network will pick up the new DNS server the next
        time they renew their DHCP lease. You can force this by disconnecting and reconnecting
        to the network, or by rebooting the device.
      </p>

      <h3>Common Router Interfaces</h3>
      <div class="table-wrapper">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Router Brand</th><th>Where to Find DNS Settings</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>ASUS</td><td>LAN → DHCP Server → DNS Server</td></tr>
            <tr><td>TP-Link</td><td>DHCP → DHCP Settings → Primary DNS</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Netgear</td><td>Internet → Domain Name Server (DNS) Address</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Linksys</td><td>Connectivity → Local Network → DHCP Server → Static DNS</td></tr>
            <tr><td>UniFi</td><td>Settings → Networks → (your network) → DHCP Name Server</td></tr>
            <tr><td>pfSense / OPNsense</td><td>Services → DHCP Server → DNS Servers</td></tr>
            <tr><td>OpenWrt</td><td>Network → Interfaces → LAN → DHCP Server → Advanced → DHCP-Options: <code>6,&lt;town-os-ip&gt;</code></td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h3>Turn Off Browser-Based DNS</h3>
      <p class="doc-text">
        <strong>Most browsers ship their own DNS resolver and bypass your router entirely.</strong>
        Firefox's DNS over HTTPS (DoH) and the equivalent "secure DNS" features in
        Chrome and Edge send lookups straight to a public provider such as Cloudflare
        or NextDNS, so Town OS never sees the query and your package names come back as
        "server not found" even though the rest of the network resolves them fine.
        Turn this off on every device that should reach your services by name:
      </p>
      <ul class="doc-list">
        <li><strong>Firefox</strong> — <strong>Settings &rarr; Privacy &amp; Security &rarr; DNS over HTTPS</strong>, choose <strong>Off</strong>. (<strong>Default Protection</strong> can also silently enable DoH, so pick Off rather than leaving it on the default.)</li>
        <li><strong>Chrome</strong> — <strong>Settings &rarr; Privacy and security &rarr; Security</strong>, turn off <strong>Use secure DNS</strong>.</li>
        <li><strong>Edge</strong> — <strong>Settings &rarr; Privacy, search, and services &rarr; Security</strong>, turn off <strong>Use secure DNS to specify how to look up network addresses</strong>.</li>
        <li><strong>Brave, Vivaldi, Opera</strong> — same setting as Chrome, under <strong>Privacy and security &rarr; Security</strong>.</li>
        <li><strong>Safari</strong> — no built-in DoH; it uses the system resolver. Check that no DNS profile is installed under <strong>System Settings &rarr; General &rarr; VPN, DNS &amp; Device Management</strong>.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="doc-text">
        The same applies to any system-wide encrypted DNS you may have configured —
        Android's <strong>Private DNS</strong>, iOS/macOS DNS profiles, or a local
        <code>systemd-resolved</code>/<code>dnscrypt</code> setup. Any of these will
        override the DNS server your router hands out. If you want encrypted upstream
        DNS, leave it to Town OS: rolodex can use DoH/DoT upstream itself while still
        answering your local names (see <a href="#resolution-mode">Choosing a Resolution Mode</a> below).
      </p>

      <h3>Verify It Works</h3>
      <p class="doc-text">
        After your device picks up the new DNS settings, verify that rolodex is handling
        your DNS queries:
      </p>
      <pre><code># Check which DNS server your machine is using
nslookup example.com

# Or query Town OS directly
dig @&lt;town-os-ip&gt; example.com

# Query a package domain (if you have packages installed).
# Names follow &lt;name&gt;.&lt;repo&gt;.&lt;tld&gt; — .home is the default network's TLD
dig @&lt;town-os-ip&gt; gitea.default.home</code></pre>
      <p class="doc-text">
        If <code>example.com</code> resolves correctly, rolodex is working
        and handling DNS for your network.
      </p>

      <h3 id="resolution-mode">Choosing a Resolution Mode</h3>
      <p class="doc-text">
        Names that don't belong to one of your networks are resolved according to the
        <strong>resolution mode</strong>, which you set under
        <strong>Settings &rarr; DNS Resolution</strong> in the dashboard. Three modes are
        available:
      </p>
      <ul class="doc-list">
        <li><strong>Automatic (recommended)</strong> — the default. Tries to resolve iteratively from the root servers first, then falls back through DoH/DoT, a local forwarder, and finally a public resolver, sticking with whichever tier last worked. This keeps recursion's privacy where the network allows it, and degrades gracefully on networks that filter outbound DNS.</li>
        <li><strong>Recursive only</strong> — resolves everything iteratively from the root servers with no fallback. Guarantees no query is ever handed to a third-party resolver, but every external name fails on networks that block or hijack outbound port 53 (hotels, captive portals, some ISPs).</li>
        <li><strong>Forward</strong> — always sends unmatched queries to upstream resolvers (Google Public DNS by default). This is the classic forwarding behavior.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 id="blocklists">Blocking Malicious Domains</h3>
      <p class="doc-text">
        <strong>rolodex can screen every query against public blocklists, giving you
        network-wide threat blocking without a separate Pi-hole.</strong> Manage this under
        <strong>DNS &rarr; Blocklists</strong>. There are two kinds of provider, and they
        work on different things:
      </p>
      <ul class="doc-list">
        <li><strong>DNSBL</strong> (domain blocklist) — matches the <em>name</em> being looked up. This is the side that affects ordinary browsing.</li>
        <li><strong>RBL</strong> (Realtime Blackhole List) — matches an <em>IP address</em>, and only on reverse DNS lookups.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="doc-text">
        Both are <strong>disabled by default with an empty provider list</strong>, and both are
        queried on demand: Town OS never downloads, parses, or pre-caches a blocklist feed.
        Nothing is checked until you turn a category on and add at least one zone.
      </p>

      <h4>How a DNSBL Lookup Works</h4>
      <p class="doc-text">
        <strong>The name being resolved is prepended to the provider's zone and looked up as an
        ordinary DNS query.</strong> Resolving <code>badsite.example</code> with
        <code>dbl.spamhaus.org</code> enabled issues a query for
        <code>badsite.example.dbl.spamhaus.org</code>.
      </p>
      <ul class="doc-list">
        <li><strong>An answer means listed</strong> — providers reply with an address record (typically <code>127.0.0.x</code>) for a listed name. rolodex then returns <code>NXDOMAIN</code> to the device that asked.</li>
        <li><strong>NXDOMAIN means clean</strong> — resolution continues normally. A provider that errors out or times out is treated as <em>not</em> listed, so an unreachable blocklist never takes your network offline.</li>
        <li><strong>Blocklists beat the outside world, never your own records</strong> — the check runs after local and package records (so <code>gitea.default.home</code> always resolves) but before the upstream cache and the forwarder, so a listed name is refused even if a forwarded answer was already cached.</li>
        <li><strong>Matching is per name, not per suffix</strong> — <code>doubleclick.net</code> being listed does not block <code>stats.g.doubleclick.net</code> unless the provider lists that name too. Taking out an entire domain over one listed host is your call to make, not the feed's.</li>
        <li><strong>Results are cached briefly</strong> — a listing for the provider's TTL, a clean result for five minutes.</li>
      </ul>

      <h4>DNSBL Providers</h4>
      <p class="doc-text">
        These are the one-click quick-adds in the dashboard. Each is still operating, free, and
        answers a self-recursing resolver with no registration step — which is exactly what a
        Town OS box is. You can also type in any other zone.
      </p>
      <div class="table-wrapper">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Provider</th><th>Zone</th><th>What it targets</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Spamhaus DBL</strong></td><td><code>dbl.spamhaus.org</code></td><td>The most widely used domain list. Domains observed in spam, plus phishing, malware-hosting, and botnet command-and-control domains. Broadest coverage of the five.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>SURBL</strong></td><td><code>multi.surbl.org</code></td><td>A combined list of domains appearing in unsolicited message bodies — phishing sites, malware, compromised sites, and abused redirectors and URL shorteners.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>URIBL</strong></td><td><code>black.uribl.com</code></td><td>URIs found in spam message bodies. The <code>black</code> zone is the conservative one: domains actively appearing in spam, with a low false-positive tolerance.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>NordSpam DBL</strong></td><td><code>dbl.nordspam.com</code></td><td>Domains seen in spam, sourced independently of the lists above — useful mainly as a second opinion rather than a primary list.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Spam Eating Monkey</strong></td><td><code>uribl.spameatingmonkey.net</code></td><td>Domains extracted from spam URIs, including newly registered and short-lived throwaway domains.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h4>How an RBL Lookup Works</h4>
      <p class="doc-text">
        <strong>The IP address is reversed and prepended to the provider's zone.</strong>
        Checking <code>192.168.1.100</code> against <code>zen.spamhaus.org</code> issues a query
        for <code>100.1.168.192.zen.spamhaus.org</code>. IPv6 addresses are expanded to nibbles
        and reversed the same way. Answer means listed, <code>NXDOMAIN</code> means clean, and
        results are cached exactly as the DNSBL ones are.
      </p>
      <p class="doc-text">
        <strong>The catch: RBL zones are only consulted for IPs found in reverse DNS
        queries</strong> (<code>in-addr.arpa</code> and <code>ip6.arpa</code>), and ordinary web
        browsing barely generates those. These lists exist to let a mail server reject a
        connecting sender, and that is where they earn their keep. On a household router they
        are close to a no-op — the DNSBL side above is what actually affects browsing. Enable
        them if you run a mail service behind Town OS; otherwise the domain lists are the ones
        worth your attention.
      </p>

      <h4>RBL Providers</h4>
      <div class="table-wrapper">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Provider</th><th>Zone</th><th>What it targets</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Spamhaus ZEN</strong></td><td><code>zen.spamhaus.org</code></td><td>Four Spamhaus lists in one query: <strong>SBL</strong> (verified spam sources), <strong>CSS</strong> (snowshoe spam operations), <strong>XBL</strong> (compromised and infected machines, open proxies), and <strong>PBL</strong> (dynamic and end-user ranges that should never send mail directly).</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>SpamCop</strong></td><td><code>bl.spamcop.net</code></td><td>IPs reported by SpamCop's user reporting network. Listings expire automatically once reports stop, so it reacts fast and forgets fast.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>PSBL</strong></td><td><code>psbl.surriel.com</code></td><td>The Passive Spam Block List: IPs caught hitting spamtrap addresses, with automatic expiry. Deliberately conservative and low-volume.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h4>Lists That Are Deliberately Not Offered</h4>
      <p class="doc-text">
        <strong>Three well-known zones are left off the quick-add list on purpose</strong>, because
        each fails <em>silently</em> — you would see a configured provider and assume you were
        protected. You can still add them by hand if you know what you are doing.
      </p>
      <ul class="doc-list">
        <li><strong>SORBS</strong> (<code>dnsbl.sorbs.net</code>) — decommissioned on 5 June 2024 and its zones emptied. It answers, it just never lists anything: permanent no-op that reads as protection.</li>
        <li><strong>Barracuda</strong> (<code>b.barracudacentral.org</code>) — free, but requires registering the querying IP first. An unregistered box may work for a while and then be cut off without notice.</li>
        <li><strong>UCEPROTECT levels 2 and 3</strong> — list entire netblocks and ASNs, so one bad neighbour on your ISP blocks the whole ISP.</li>
      </ul>

      <h4>Local Entries and Allow Lists</h4>
      <ul class="doc-list">
        <li><strong>Local entries</strong> (<strong>DNS &rarr; Blocklists</strong>) — block a specific domain or IP by hand, with a reason recorded alongside it. A domain entry returns <code>NXDOMAIN</code> for forward lookups and takes effect immediately. This is checked before any external provider, and is how you block one specific host without signing up to a whole feed.</li>
        <li><strong>Allow lists</strong> (<strong>DNS &rarr; Allow Lists</strong>) — your escape hatch from a false positive. An allowlisted name short-circuits the entire name-based check: it is tested against neither the DNSBL providers nor your local entries, and no provider query is issued for it at all. Unlike blocking, allowing <em>is</em> suffix-matched — exempting <code>vendor.example</code> also exempts <code>cdn.vendor.example</code>. Entries are names only, never IPs, so the reverse-IP RBL path is untouched by them.</li>
      </ul>

      <h4>What to Expect</h4>
      <p class="doc-text">
        <strong>These feeds are built for email, not for advertising.</strong> They are excellent
        at phishing, malware, and spam payload domains, and mediocre at ads and trackers — that
        job belongs to downloadable hosts-style feeds, which Town OS deliberately does not
        ingest. Nothing is fetched on a schedule, nothing is parsed, and nothing is cached behind
        your back. If you want a particular ad or tracker domain gone, add it as a local entry.
      </p>

      <h3>Publishing Services in DNS</h3>
      <p class="doc-text">
        By default every installed package service is published in DNS under its
        <a href="#networks">network</a>'s TLD
        so you can reach it by name. Under <strong>DNS &rarr; Services</strong> you can toggle
        publishing per service — an unpublished service keeps running but is no longer resolvable
        by name. Each service's fully-qualified name follows the pattern
        <code>&lt;name&gt;.&lt;repo&gt;.&lt;tld&gt;</code> (for the default network, that TLD is
        <code>.home</code>).
      </p>

      <DiagramFlow title="DNS Flow">
        <DiagramBox label="Device" sub="Phone, laptop, etc." variant="neutral">
          <DiagramTag>DNS query</DiagramTag>
        </DiagramBox>
        <DiagramArrow label="DHCP" />
        <DiagramBox label="Router" sub="Hands out Town OS as DNS" variant="neutral">
          <DiagramTag>DNS = Town OS IP</DiagramTag>
        </DiagramBox>
        <DiagramArrow label="forward" />
        <DiagramBox label="Town OS" sub="rolodex DNS server" variant="purple">
          <DiagramTag variant="teal">Authoritative zones</DiagramTag>
          <DiagramTag variant="teal">Upstream forwarding</DiagramTag>
        </DiagramBox>
      </DiagramFlow>
    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- Trusting the CA -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <h2 class="section-title" id="trusting-the-ca">Trusting the Certificate Authority</h2>
      <p class="doc-text">
        Town OS issues TLS certificates for your internal services through the
        <a href="https://gitea.com/town-os/rolodex" target="_blank" rel="noopener">rolodex</a>
        built-in certificate authority. For your browser to show a padlock instead of a
        warning, each device needs to trust the <strong>Rolodex root CA</strong> once.
        There are three ways to fetch it &mdash; pick whichever fits the device.
      </p>

      <h3>Option 1: From DNS (works anywhere DNS does)</h3>
      <p class="doc-text">
        Rolodex publishes the CA chain <em>in DNS itself</em>, so any device that can
        resolve your zone can fetch the CA &mdash; no access to the enrollment portal
        required. The root and per-zone intermediate are served as
        <code>CERT</code> records (RFC&nbsp;4398) at <code>_ca.&lt;zone&gt;</code>, with a
        chunked <code>TXT</code> fallback at <code>_rolodex-ca.&lt;zone&gt;</code>:
      </p>
      <pre><code># See the published CA records
dig @&lt;town-os-ip&gt; CERT _ca.example.home

# Extract the root CA to a PEM file (root = the self-signed one)
dig @&lt;town-os-ip&gt; +short CERT _ca.example.home</code></pre>
      <p class="doc-text">
        The easiest way to consume these records in a browser is the
        <strong>Rolodex browser extension</strong> (in the rolodex repository under
        <code>extension/</code>, loaded unpacked via <code>chrome://extensions</code> or
        <code>about:debugging</code> in Firefox). Open the popup&rsquo;s
        <strong>CA via DNS</strong> section, enter your DoH URL
        (<code>https://&lt;town-os-ip&gt;/dns-query</code>) and zone, and it retrieves the
        chain over DNS-over-HTTPS, prefers the CERT records with automatic TXT fallback,
        verifies it against the published DANE TLSA records if you give it a hostname,
        and offers the root, intermediate, and full chain as PEM downloads.
      </p>

      <h3>Option 2: From the Enrollment Portal</h3>
      <p class="doc-text">
        On the trusted network, browse to the enrollment portal
        (<code>https://&lt;town-os-ip&gt;:8500</code> by default) and click
        <strong>Download root CA (PEM)</strong>. The same extension and the
        <code>rolodex-ca-ui</code> local console can do this too.
      </p>

      <h3>Option 3: From the Command Line</h3>
      <pre><code># Via the management CLI (prints root + intermediate PEM)
rolodex-dns-cli ensure-zone-ca --zone example.home

# Or fetch the portal download directly
curl -k https://&lt;town-os-ip&gt;:8500/api/ca -o rolodex-root-ca.pem</code></pre>

      <h3>Install the Root CA on Your Device</h3>
      <p class="doc-text">
        Once you have <code>rolodex-root-ca.pem</code>, add it to the trust store:
      </p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Platform</th><th>How</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Firefox</td><td>Settings &rarr; Privacy &amp; Security &rarr; Certificates &rarr; View Certificates &rarr; Authorities &rarr; Import (check &ldquo;Trust this CA to identify websites&rdquo;)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Chrome / Edge (desktop)</td><td>Uses the OS trust store &mdash; install per the OS rows below, then restart the browser</td></tr>
            <tr><td>macOS</td><td>Double-click the PEM to add it to Keychain Access, then set it to &ldquo;Always Trust&rdquo; under SSL</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Linux (Fedora/RHEL)</td><td><code>sudo cp rolodex-root-ca.pem /etc/pki/ca-trust/source/anchors/ &amp;&amp; sudo update-ca-trust</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Linux (Debian/Ubuntu)</td><td><code>sudo cp rolodex-root-ca.pem /usr/local/share/ca-certificates/rolodex.crt &amp;&amp; sudo update-ca-certificates</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Windows</td><td>Double-click &rarr; Install Certificate &rarr; Local Machine &rarr; &ldquo;Trusted Root Certification Authorities&rdquo;</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Android</td><td>Settings &rarr; Security &rarr; Encryption &amp; credentials &rarr; Install a certificate &rarr; CA certificate</td></tr>
            <tr><td>iOS</td><td>Open the PEM (AirDrop/mail), install the profile, then enable it under Settings &rarr; General &rarr; About &rarr; Certificate Trust Settings</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="doc-text">
        Servers issued through the Rolodex ACME endpoint present a
        <code>leaf + intermediate</code> chain that validates against this root. DANE-aware
        clients can additionally verify the intermediate against the
        <code>TLSA</code> records rolodex publishes automatically on issuance.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Networks and Remote Access -->
  <section class="section alt-section">
    <div class="container">
      <h2 class="section-title" id="networks">Networks and Remote Access</h2>
      <p class="doc-text">
        Town OS groups your services into <strong>networks</strong>. Every box starts with a
        built-in network called <code>home</code> — it is LAN-only, resolves names under
        <code>.home</code>, and has no tunnel. To reach your services securely from outside your
        house, create additional networks: each one is a <strong>WireGuard overlay</strong>
        paired with its own DNS TLD, managed under <strong>Dashboard &rarr; Networks</strong>
        (admin only).
      </p>
      <div class="screenshot-ref">
        <img src={`${base}images/screenshots/networks-panel.webp`} alt="The Networks page listing the built-in home network with its TLD, subnet, peer count, and a Remote access toggle, plus a Create Network button" width="1920" height="891" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
      </div>

      <h3>The Default Network</h3>
      <ul class="doc-list">
        <li><strong>Always present, can't be removed.</strong> The <code>home</code> network is created automatically and is where packages land unless you pick another.</li>
        <li><strong>Local only.</strong> It has no WireGuard transport — <code>.home</code> names resolve on your LAN and are deliberately never exposed to remote peers.</li>
        <li><strong>Uses the <code>.home</code> TLD</strong>, which comes from the <code>dns_tld</code> setting.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 id="creating-a-network">Creating a Network for Remote Access</h3>
      <p class="doc-text">
        Click <strong>Create Network</strong> and give it a name (for example <code>office</code>)
        and, optionally, a TLD (it defaults to the network name). Town OS then:
      </p>
      <ul class="doc-list">
        <li>Generates a WireGuard interface with a deterministic overlay subnet drawn from the <code>10.64.0.0/10</code> range, with the box itself as the <code>.1</code> address.</li>
        <li>Claims the network's TLD (TLDs are unique per box — creating a network with a TLD another network already owns is rejected).</li>
        <li>Runs a per-network DNS resolver on the overlay so joined devices can resolve that network's names.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="screenshot-ref">
        <img src={`${base}images/screenshots/networks-create.webp`} alt="The Create Network dialog with Name and TLD fields, noting the TLD defaults to the network name" width="798" height="540" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
      </div>
      <p class="doc-text">
        The <strong>Remote access</strong> switch on each row brings the WireGuard interface up or
        down. Turning it off cuts remote access while leaving the containers running and reachable
        locally.
      </p>

      <h3>Enrolling a Device</h3>
      <p class="doc-text">
        Open a network's <strong>Peers</strong> dialog, enter a device name, and click
        <strong>Add Peer</strong>. Town OS returns a ready-to-import WireGuard configuration —
        paste it into the WireGuard app on your phone or laptop.
      </p>
      <p class="doc-text callout">
        <strong>Copy the config right away.</strong> It contains a freshly generated private key
        that is never stored — you cannot retrieve it later, and will have to enroll the device
        again if you lose it.
      </p>
      <p class="doc-text">
        The generated config points the device's DNS at the box's overlay address, so your
        network's names resolve automatically once the tunnel is up. Leave the
        <strong>Runs rolodex DNS</strong> switch off for ordinary devices; enable it only for a
        peer that itself runs a rolodex DNS server you want to forward to.
      </p>

      <h3>WireGuard-Only Accounts</h3>
      <p class="doc-text">
        To let someone enroll their own devices without giving them run of the dashboard, create a
        <strong>WireGuard-only account</strong> (a checkbox on the Create User screen). Such an
        account:
      </p>
      <ul class="doc-list">
        <li>Is <strong>scoped to one or more specific networks</strong> — it can enroll peers only on those, and never on the <code>home</code> network.</li>
        <li>Is <strong>fail-closed</strong>: it can authenticate, enroll and refresh its own peers, and fetch the CA, but nothing else in the control plane.</li>
        <li>Enrolls <strong>expiring peers</strong>. Each enrollment carries a TTL (default 2 hours, set under <strong>Settings &rarr; WireGuard Peer TTL</strong>) and must be refreshed to stay connected; abandoned devices age out automatically. Peers an admin adds are permanent.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Watching and Disconnecting Peers</h3>
      <p class="doc-text">
        The <strong>Connected Peers</strong> panel on the Networks page lists every enrolled peer
        across all networks with its live handshake status, overlay IP, data transferred, and
        expiry. To forcibly cut a device off, use <strong>Disconnect</strong> — this removes the
        peer, tears its tunnel down immediately, and revokes its key, so the device cannot
        reconnect until it is enrolled again.
      </p>

      <h3 id="installing-packages-on-a-network">Installing Packages on a Network</h3>
      <p class="doc-text">
        The install dialog (and the <a href="#pages">Pages</a> dialog) includes a
        <strong>Network</strong> selector.
        Whichever network you choose determines the service's DNS name and
        <a href="#trusting-the-ca">TLS certificate</a> — a
        package installed on <code>office</code> resolves under <code>.office</code> and its
        certificate is issued for that name. Services on a non-default network are dual-homed: they
        resolve to the overlay address for tunnelled peers and to the LAN address for local
        clients. Reinstalling a package on a different network moves its DNS and certificates to
        that network's TLD.
      </p>
      <div class="screenshot-ref">
        <img src={`${base}images/screenshots/networks-install.webp`} alt="The Install jitsi dialog with a Network selector at the top labeled 'The network this package is served on', above the package's configuration questions" width="768" height="625" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
      </div>
      <p class="doc-text">
        For example, installing Jitsi on a network with the <code>fart</code> TLD publishes it at
        <code>jitsi.default.fart</code>. Once it's running, the service shows up on your
        <strong>Dashboard</strong> as a clickable link at that address, ready to open from any
        device on the network.
      </p>
      <div class="screenshot-ref">
        <img src={`${base}images/screenshots/networks-dashboard.webp`} alt="The Dashboard's Installed Services list showing jitsi with a link to https://jitsi.default.fart/" width="1546" height="414" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
      </div>

      <DiagramFlow title="Remote Access over a Network">
        <DiagramBox label="Your Device" sub="Phone or laptop" variant="neutral">
          <DiagramTag>WireGuard</DiagramTag>
        </DiagramBox>
        <DiagramArrow label="tunnel" />
        <DiagramBox label="Town OS" sub="Network overlay + DNS" variant="purple">
          <DiagramTag variant="purple">office subnet</DiagramTag>
          <DiagramTag variant="teal">.office TLD</DiagramTag>
        </DiagramBox>
        <DiagramArrow label="resolve" />
        <DiagramBox label="Your Services" sub="Reachable by name" variant="teal">
          <DiagramTag variant="teal">gitea.default.office</DiagramTag>
        </DiagramBox>
      </DiagramFlow>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Town OS on Android -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <h2 class="section-title" id="android-app">Town OS on Android</h2>
      <p class="doc-text">
        The <a href="https://github.com/town-os/android-client" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Town OS
        Android client</a> connects your phone to one of your
        <a href="#networks">networks</a> over WireGuard and sets up DNS so your services resolve by
        name — from anywhere. It is a full WireGuard client that enrolls the phone as a peer for
        you; there is no config file to copy by hand.
      </p>

      <div class="phone-shots">
        <figure class="phone-shot">
          <img src={`${base}images/screenshots/android-login.webp`} alt="Town OS Android client login screen — a Connect to a box card with box address, admin username, and password fields" width="1080" height="2424" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
          <figcaption>Logging in to a box</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="phone-shot">
          <img src={`${base}images/screenshots/android-connection.webp`} alt="Town OS Android client connection screen — Disconnected status with Connect and Forget this network buttons, above a DNS resolver settings card" width="1080" height="2424" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
          <figcaption>Managing the connection and DNS</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>

      <h3>Installing the App</h3>
      <p class="doc-text">
        The app is distributed as an APK from the project's
        <a href="https://github.com/town-os/android-client/releases" target="_blank" rel="noopener">releases page</a>
        — it is not on the Play Store or F-Droid.
      </p>
      <ul class="doc-list">
        <li>Download the <strong>debug APK</strong> (<code>town-os-client-&lt;version&gt;-debug.apk</code>). The <code>-unsigned.apk</code> on the same release cannot be installed as-is — the project ships no signing key, so the debug build is the one to grab.</li>
        <li>Requires <strong>Android 8.0 (Oreo) or newer</strong>.</li>
        <li>Because it is sideloaded, allow <strong>installing unknown apps</strong> when your browser or file manager prompts.</li>
        <li>Prefer to build it yourself? Clone the repo and run <code>make deps &amp;&amp; make debug &amp;&amp; make install</code> with the phone connected over USB and USB debugging enabled. <code>make help</code> lists every target.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="doc-text">To install the downloaded APK on your phone:</p>
      <ol class="doc-list">
        <li><strong>Get the APK onto the phone.</strong> Download it directly in the phone's browser, or copy it over from a computer via USB or <code>adb push</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Open the APK</strong> from your browser's downloads or a file manager. The first time, Android asks to <strong>allow installs from this source</strong> — grant it (<strong>Settings &rarr; Apps &rarr; Special access &rarr; Install unknown apps</strong>), then tap <strong>Install</strong>.</li>
        <li><strong>Or install from a computer</strong> with the phone in USB-debugging mode: <code>adb install town-os-client-&lt;version&gt;-debug.apk</code>.</li>
        <li>Open <strong>Town OS</strong> from your app drawer once it finishes installing.</li>
      </ol>

      <h4>Enabling Developer Mode</h4>
      <p class="doc-text">
        You only need this for the <code>adb</code> route — installing the APK from the phone's own
        downloads works without it. Both <code>adb install</code> and <code>make install</code> talk
        to the phone over USB debugging, which lives behind Android's hidden
        <strong>Developer options</strong> menu.
      </p>
      <ol class="doc-list">
        <li>Open <strong>Settings &rarr; About phone</strong>. On Samsung phones it is <strong>Settings &rarr; About phone &rarr; Software information</strong>.</li>
        <li>Tap <strong>Build number</strong> seven times. Android counts down ("You are now 3 steps away from being a developer") and asks for your PIN, pattern, or password before it finishes.</li>
        <li>A message confirms <strong>You are now a developer</strong>. <strong>Developer options</strong> then appears under <strong>Settings &rarr; System</strong> — some phones put it at the top level of Settings instead, so search Settings for it if it isn't where you expect.</li>
        <li>Open <strong>Developer options</strong> and turn on <strong>USB debugging</strong>.</li>
        <li>Plug the phone into the computer. Android shows an <em>Allow USB debugging?</em> dialog with the computer's key fingerprint — tick <strong>Always allow from this computer</strong> and accept it. Run <code>adb devices</code> to confirm the phone is listed as <code>device</code> rather than <code>unauthorized</code>.</li>
      </ol>
      <p class="doc-text">
        Turn <strong>USB debugging</strong> back off when you are done. It gives every computer you
        have authorized full access to the phone over the debug bridge, so leaving it on for daily
        use is a needless risk.
      </p>

      <h3>Before You Connect</h3>
      <ul class="doc-list">
        <li><strong>Create a network first.</strong> The app can only join networks that carry a WireGuard overlay — the built-in <code>home</code> network is LAN-only and won't appear. See <a href="#creating-a-network">Creating a Network for Remote Access</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Have an administrator account ready.</strong> Enrolling a device is an admin action, so the app signs in with admin credentials.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Connecting to a Network</h3>
      <ol class="doc-list">
        <li><strong>Log in to your box.</strong> Enter its address — a bare IP, <code>IP:port</code>, or full URL (port <code>5309</code> is assumed) — along with your admin username and password.</li>
        <li><strong>Join a network.</strong> The app lists the networks you can join, each with its TLD, subnet, and peer count. Give the device a name (it defaults to your phone's model) and tap <strong>Join</strong>. The WireGuard keypair is generated on the phone and only the public half is sent to the box, so your private key never leaves the device.</li>
        <li><strong>Connect.</strong> Tap <strong>Connect</strong> and approve Android's <em>Connection request</em> (VPN) prompt. The app brings up the tunnel and installs the network's TLD as a search domain, so both <code>gitea</code> and <code>gitea.default.&lt;tld&gt;</code> resolve.</li>
        <li>You are now on the network by name from anywhere. Use <strong>Disconnect</strong> to drop the tunnel, or <strong>Forget this network</strong> to remove the saved enrollment.</li>
      </ol>

      <h3>Troubleshooting</h3>
      <ul class="doc-list">
        <li><strong>Names don't resolve, but the tunnel is up.</strong> The usual culprit is Android's strict <strong>Private DNS</strong>. If it is set to a specific provider hostname, Android sends every lookup there and ignores the tunnel, so Town OS names come back "not found." Switch <strong>Settings &rarr; Network &amp; internet &rarr; Private DNS</strong> to <strong>Automatic</strong> or <strong>Off</strong>. The app detects this and shows a warning with a shortcut straight to the setting. (Automatic mode is fine and never warns.)</li>
        <li><strong>Still nothing?</strong> In the app's DNS card, set the <strong>resolver override</strong> to the box's LAN address — the app routes it through the tunnel so split-horizon DNS still works.</li>
        <li><strong>Logged out after the box reboots.</strong> Town OS clears all sessions on restart; just log in again.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Building VMs -->
  <section class="section alt-section">
    <div class="container">
      <h2 class="section-title" id="building-vms">Building VMs from USB Images</h2>
      <p class="doc-text">
        The <a href="https://gitea.com/town-os/install" target="_blank" rel="noopener">install</a>
        repository includes scripts for launching Town OS in virtual machines, which is useful
        for testing and development — either from an image you
        <a href="#building-the-usb-image">built from source</a> or straight from a
        <a href="#qemu-usb">physical USB stick you already flashed</a>.
        <code>make help</code> lists every target and variable.
      </p>

      <h3 id="qemu">QEMU</h3>
      <pre><code>make qemu-fg</code></pre>
      <p class="doc-text">
        Builds the image if it is stale, then launches a QEMU VM in the <strong>foreground</strong>
        with the serial console attached, KVM acceleration, and four virtual data disks for storage
        testing. This is the target to reach for: you watch the boot and the installer directly in
        your terminal, and Ctrl-C stops the VM. Use <code>make qemu</code> if you would rather it run
        in the background (attach later with <a href="#serial-console"><code>make serial</code></a>),
        and <code>make rebuild-qemu</code> to stop, clean, rebuild, and relaunch in one step.
      </p>
      <p class="doc-text">
        The VM attaches to the libvirt <code>default</code> NAT network on <code>virbr0</code> and is
        pinned to <code>VM_IP</code> (<code>192.168.122.50</code> by default), so give each
        concurrently running VM its own address.
      </p>
      <p class="doc-text">
        Because the guest is NAT'd, <code>VM_LAN=1</code> (the default) relays the control API
        (<code>5309</code>), the UI (<code>80</code>/<code>443</code>), ssh
        (<code>2222</code>), and the WireGuard UDP ports from the host's LAN address into the
        guest — that is what lets a phone running the
        <a href="#android-app">Android client</a> reach a VM. Set <code>VM_LAN=0</code> to turn
        the relays off.
      </p>

      <h3 id="qemu-usb">Booting a Physical USB (qemu-usb)</h3>
      <pre><code>make qemu-usb USB_DEV=/dev/sdX</code></pre>
      <p class="doc-text">
        Boots QEMU in the foreground straight from a flashed physical USB stick instead
        of a built image — handy for confirming that a stick you just wrote actually boots.
        The device is opened <strong>read-only</strong> (snapshot), so guest writes are
        discarded and the real USB is never modified; the four virtual data disks are still
        attached for storage testing. This target does not build anything — clone the install
        repository, flash a stick with <code>install.sh</code> or <code>make flash</code>, then
        point <code>USB_DEV</code> at it. On an x86_64 host,
        <code>TARGET=aarch64</code> (or <code>rpi</code>) boots the stick under full-system
        emulation, so a foreign-arch image can be tested without the matching hardware.
      </p>

      <h3 id="stopping-vms">Stopping and Cleaning Up</h3>
      <pre><code># Stop the VM
make stop

# Stop VMs and delete the image and virtual disks
make clean</code></pre>

      <h3 id="vm-environment-variables">Environment Variables</h3>
      <div class="table-wrapper">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Variable</th><th>Default</th><th>Description</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>IMAGE_SIZE</code></td><td>12G</td><td>Sparse build size of the USB image — the image is shrunk afterwards</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>VM_DISK_SIZE</code></td><td>50G</td><td>Size of each of the four virtual data disks (read from <code>vm_disk_size</code> in <code>town-os.yaml</code>)</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>VM_MEMORY</code></td><td>4G</td><td>VM memory</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>VM_CPUS</code></td><td>4</td><td>vCPUs for the VM — QEMU's default of 1 starves rolodex's worker pool</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>VM_BRIDGE</code></td><td>virbr0</td><td>Host bridge interface for networking</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>VM_NAME</code></td><td>town-os</td><td>VM name, also used to find and stop it</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>VM_IP</code></td><td>192.168.122.50</td><td>libvirt DHCP reservation — give each concurrently running VM its own address</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>VM_LAN</code></td><td>1</td><td>Relay the NAT'd guest's ports to the host's LAN address; <code>0</code> disables</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>USB_DEV</code></td><td>—</td><td>Physical block device for <code>make flash</code> (write) and <code>make qemu-usb</code> (read-only boot)</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h3 id="serial-console">Serial Console</h3>
      <pre><code># Attach to the VM serial console
make serial

# Or manually via socat
socat -,rawer,escape=0x1d unix-connect:/tmp/town-os-serial.sock

# Detach with Ctrl-]</code></pre>

      <h3 id="finding-the-vm">Finding the VM</h3>
      <pre><code># Get the VM's IP address
make vm-ip

# Or connect via mDNS from the host
ssh root@town-os.local</code></pre>

